---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { classes, classUrl, url, REPO_URL } from "../lib/data.js";

const unconfirmed = classes.filter((c) => c.verified === false);
const unknown = classes.filter((c) => !c.faction);
---
<Base title="About" description="Where Edicts' patch notes come from and how they're tagged.">
  <h1>About</h1>
  <p>
    Edicts puts the patch notes for Throne of Lies, Imperium42's social deduction game,
    in one place where you can read them by patch, by class or by category. It's a fan
    project with no connection to Imperium42, and the notes themselves are theirs.
  </p>

  <h2>Sources</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      The official forum (forum.imperium42.com, later forum.throneoflies.com), read from the{" "}
      <a href="https://fol-archive.netlify.app/">Forum of Lies archive</a>. It covers the
      alpha in 2016 up to v5.0.2 in September 2021.
    </li>
    <li>The game's announcements on Steam, the only source after September 2021.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    When a release was posted in both places, the forum text is shown and the Steam post
    is linked beside it. Announcements that aren't patch notes are in the{" "}
    <a href={url("/chronicle/")}>Chronicle</a>.
  </p>
  <p>
    Pictures in the patch notes are shown as [image] links to the original files, which
    keeps pages small; most were large animated banners. Chronicle posts such as class
    reveals show their pictures.
  </p>

  <h2>How changes are tagged</h2>
  <p>
    Each post is split into its bullet points. A change is tagged with a class when it sits
    under that class's heading, or names the class or one of its abilities. It gets one{" "}
    <a href={url("/categories/")}>category</a> from its heading or wording. A script does this
    with a list of hand corrections, so a few tags will be wrong. Introductions, summaries
    and forum replies are left out of the lists but are still in each patch's notes.
  </p>
  {REPO_URL && <p>Spotted a wrong tag?{" "}<a href={`${REPO_URL}/issues/new?template=wrong-tag.yml`}>Report it on GitHub</a>.</p>}

  <h2>Factions</h2>
  <p>
    The notes rarely say which faction a class belongs to, so the factions and convert
    links come from players who know the game, checked against the notes where they say.
    {unconfirmed.length > 0 && <> These are still best guesses and may be wrong:{" "}
      {unconfirmed.map((c, i) => <>{i > 0 && ", "}<a href={classUrl(c.name)}>{c.name}</a></>)}.</>}
    {unknown.length > 0 && <> No faction is known for {unknown.map((c, i) => <>{i > 0 && ", "}<a href={classUrl(c.name)}>{c.name}</a></>)}, which appears only in the late notes.</>}
  </p>
</Base>
